{"step_index":120,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:40:14Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:40:14+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:40:14+02:00\n\nThe command exited with code 0.\nOutput:\n\r\n\\noindent The definitions of $2$-rainbow domination on the one hand and Roman domination on the other hand clearly exhibit certain similarities.\r\nIt is therefore not surprising that these notions are related.\r\nFor later reference, we include the simple proof of the following known results.\r\n\r\n\\begin{theorem}[Wu and Xing \\cite{wx}, Chellali and Rad \\cite{cr}, and Fujita and Furuya \\cite{ff}]\\label{theorembefore}\r\nIf $G$ is a graph, then $\\gamma_{r2}(G)\\leq \\gamma_R(G)\\leq \\frac{3}{2}\\gamma_{r2}(G)$.\r\n\\end{theorem}\r\n{\\it Proof:} These inequalities follow immediately from two simple observations:\r\nIf $g$ is a Roman dominating function of $G$, then \r\n$$f:V(G)\\to 2^{\\{ 1,2\\}}: x\\mapsto\r\n\\begin{cases}\r\n\\emptyset & \\mbox{, if $g(x)=0$,}\\\\\r\n\\{ 1\\} & \\mbox{, if $g(x)=1$, and}\\\\\r\n\\{ 1,2\\} & \\mbox{, if $g(x)=2$}\r\n\\end{cases}\r\n$$\r\nis a $2$-rainbow dominating function of $G$ of weight $w(f)\\leq w(g)$.\r\nSimilarly, if $f$ is a $2$-rainbow dominating function of $G$, \r\nand $|f^{-1}(\\{ 1\\})|\\geq |f^{-1}(\\{ 2\\})|$, then\r\n$$g:V(G)\\to \\{ 0,1,2\\}: x\\mapsto\r\n\\begin{cases}\r\n0 & \\mbox{, if $f(x)=\\emptyset$,}\\\\\r\n1 & \\mbox{, if $f(x)=\\{ 1\\}$, and}\\\\\r\n2 & \\mbox{, otherwise}\r\n\r\n\\begin{theorem}\\label{theorem2}\r\nA graph $G$ satisfies $\\gamma_{r2}(H)=\\gamma_R(H)$ for every induced subgraph $H$ of $G$ if and only if $G$ is $\\{P_5,C_5,C_4\\}$-free. \r\n\\end{theorem}\r\n{\\it Proof:} Since $\\gamma_{r2}(H)<\\gamma_R(H)$ for every graph $H$ in $\\{P_5,C_5,C_4\\}$, the necessity follows immediately.\r\nIn view of Theorem \\ref{theorembefore}, \r\nin order to complete the proof, \r\nit suffices to show \r\nthat every $\\{P_5,C_5,C_4\\}$-free graph $G$ satisfies $\\gamma_R(G)\\leq \\gamma_{r2}(G)$.\r\nTherefore, let $G$ be a $\\{P_5,C_5,C_4\\}$-free graph, \r\nand let $f$ be a minimum $2$-rainbow dominating function of $G$\r\nsuch that $|f^{-1}(\\{ 1,2\\})|$ is as large as possible.\r\nFor $F\\in 2^{\\{ 1,2\\}}$, let $V_{F}=f^{-1}(F)$.\r\nLet\r\n$$g:V(G)\\to \\{ 0,1,2\\}:x\\map\n<truncated 434 bytes>\n\\end{theorem}\r\n{\\it Proof:}\r\nSince $\\gamma_{r2}(\\bar{K}_3)=\\gamma_R(\\bar{K}_3)=\\gamma_{r2}(K_2\\cup K_1)=\\gamma_R(K_2\\cup K_1)=3$, \r\nthe graphs in ${\\cal G}_3\\left(\\gamma_{R},\\frac{3}{2}\\gamma_{r2}\\right)$ are $\\{\\bar{K}_3, K_2\\cup K_1\\}$-free.\r\nIn view of Theorem \\ref{theorembefore}, \r\nin order to complete the proof, it suffices to show \r\nthat every $\\{\\bar{K}_3, K_2\\cup K_1\\}$-free graph $G$ with $\\gamma_{r2}(G)\\geq 3$ \r\nsatisfies $\\gamma_{R}(G)=\\frac{3}{2}\\gamma_{r2}(G)$.\r\nTherefore, let $G$ be a $\\{\\bar{K}_3, K_2\\cup K_1\\}$-free graph with $\\gamma_{r2}(G)\\geq 3$.\r\nSince $\\gamma_{r2}(G)\\geq 3$, the graph $G$ is not complete.\r\nLet $u$ and $v$ be two distinct vertices of $G$ that are not adjacent.\r\nSince $\\{\\bar{K}_3, K_2\\cup K_1\\}$-free, we obtain $N_G(u)=N_G(v)=V(G)\\setminus \\{ u,v\\}$.\r\nThis implies that\r\n$$x\\mapsto\r\n\\begin{cases}\r\n\\{ 1\\} &\\mbox{, $x=u$,}\\\\\r\n\\{ 2\\} & \\mbox{, $x=v$, and}\\\\\r\n\\emptyset & \\mbox{, otherwise}\r\n\\end{cases}\r\n$$\r\ndefines a $2$-rainbow dominating function of $G$ of weight $2$, which is a contradiction. $\\Box$\r\n\r\n\\medskip\r\n\r\n{\\it Proof:} Let $G$ be a graph with $\\gamma_R(G)= \\frac{3}{2}\\gamma_{r2}(G)$, and let $f$ be a minimum $2$-rainbow dominating function of $G$.\r\n\r\n\\medskip\r\n\r\n\\noindent (i) Since the inequality (\\ref{e1}) \r\nin the proof of Theorem \\ref{theorembefore}\r\nis satisfied with equality, \r\nwe obtain $n_{\\{ 1\\}}=n_{\\{ 2\\}}$.\r\nSimilarly, since (\\ref{e2}) is satisfied with equality, \r\nwe obtain $n_{\\{ 1,2\\}}=0$.\r\nSince $f$ is a $2$-rainbow dominating function of $G$ and $n_{\\{ 1,2\\}}=0$,\r\nevery vertex in $V_{\\emptyset}$ has a neighbor in $V_{\\{ 1\\}}$ as well as a neighbor in $V_{\\{ 2\\}}$.\r\n\r\n\\medskip\r\n\r\n\\noindent (ii) If $v_1$ in $V_{\\{ 1\\}}$ is adjacent to $v_2$ in $V_{\\{ 2\\}}$, then \r\n$$\r\nx\\mapsto\r\n\\begin{cases}\r\n0 & \\mbox{, $x\\in \\{ v_2\\}\\cup V_{\\emptyset}$,}\\\\\r\n1 & \\mbox{, $x\\in V_{\\{ 2\\}}\\setminus \\{ v_2\\}$, and}\\\\\r\n2 & \\mbox{, $x\\in V_{\\{ 1\\}}$}\r\n\\end{cases}\r\n$$\r\ndefines a Roman dominating function of $G$ of weight $\\frac{3}{2}\\gamma_{r2}(G)-1$, which is a contradiction.\r\n\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":121,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:40:14Z","thinking":"**Confirming inequalities and citations**\n\nIt's established that $\\gamma_{r2}(G) \\le \\gamma_R(G)$ always holds. Considering $\\gamma_t(G) \\le \\gamma_{r2}(G)$, this was conjectured by Chellali, Haynes, and Hedetniemi (2014) and confirmed by Furuya in 2015. Need to locate Furuya's paper to verify details. Searching for citations of Furuya (2015) might provide a link.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c \\\"\\nimport urllib.request, xml.etree.ElementTree as ET\\n\\nurl = 'http://export.arxiv.org/api/query?search_query=all:%22Furuya%22+AND+all:%22total+domination%22&start=0&max_results=10'\\nreq = urllib.request.Request(url, headers={'User-Agent': 'Mozilla/5.0'})\\nwith urllib.request.urlopen(req) as resp:\\n    content = resp.read().decode('utf-8')\\nroot = ET.fromstring(content)\\nfor entry in root.findall('{http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom}entry'):\\n    print(entry.find('{http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom}title').text.strip())\\n    print(entry.find('{http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom}id').text.strip())\\n\\\"\"","Cwd":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_40a99333\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Searching arXiv for Furuya total domination\"","toolSummary":"\"Search arXiv Furuya total dom\""}}]}
